raphael (Redmi K20 Pro, SM8150): the vibration motor and the flashlight, ported from HakiOS.

- Source: HakiOS's raphael drivers (drivers/haptic/hakios.raphael_haptic.c, the raphael path of
  drivers/camera/hakios.torch.c), which work on this phone under HakiOS's xnu-8796 kernel. Rewritten onto
  sweet_spmi.c instead of HakiOS's own PMIC arbiter code: same registers, values and order of writes, plus the
  haptics actuator type (below), which HakiOS does not set.
- Vibration: PM8150B haptics (SID 3 0xc000, an ERM). Constant VMAX drive at 2552 mV (device tree vmax 3000 mV):
  CFG1 0xc04c bit 0 = ERM (the block resets to LRA; after `fastboot boot' Android's qti-haptics never set it, and
  without it the motor did not buzz on the phone; with it, it does), VMAX_CFG 0xc051, SEL 0xc04e (source VMAX), EN_CTL1 0xc046,
  PLAY 0xc070 (qti-haptics.c order); at most 1000 ms
  a buzz; a short circuit (STATUS1 0xc00a bit 3) stops it and latches it off for the boot.
- Torch: PM8150L flash v2 (SID 5 0xd300), channel 0 only, at most 100 mA: IRESOLUTION 0xd347, STROBE_CTRL0 0xd349,
  ITARGET0 0xd343, SAFETY_TIMER0 0xd340 (0x13), MODULE_EN 0xd346, EN_LED_CTRL 0xd34c (leds-qpnp-flash-v2.c order).
  Off after sweettorch_max_s (default 30, max 120); the module's fault flag (0xd310), supply droop (0xd309 bit 4)
  or the PM8150L temperature alarm (SID 5 0x2408) switch it off and latch it.
- Every write but PLAY is read back; a failed or mismatched write latches that part off (the off writes still go).
- sweet_spmi.c: raphael reads gain SID 3 0xc0 and SID 5 0xd3 / 0x24; raphael writes gain exactly those eleven
  single-byte registers, and only after sweet_spmi_vibtorch_writes() (sweetvib= / sweettorch= >= 2).
- Boot-args (all off by default): sweetvib=1 / sweettorch=1 log the blocks (reads only); =2 one test at boot
  (sweetvib_ms, default 150; sweettorch_s, default 2); =3 serve kern.sweet_vibrate (ms) and kern.sweet_torch
  (0..1000 permille of 100 mA). sweet_reboot_restart() switches both off first; on a panic,
  sweet_reboot_note_panic() calls sweet_vibtorch_panic(), which switches both off with SPMI in atomic mode (the
  write channels are already mapped). Without it, a panic during the boot buzz left the motor running.
- kern.sweet_vibtorch_state (read-only string): both parts' level, type/subtype, latch and its reason, and why the
  last probe read failed (sweet_spmi_read_why(), new in sweet_spmi.c). The probe is tried 10 times at start-up and
  again when a buzz is asked for: on two iOS boots the first read of the haptics block failed, and the first
  version then switched vibration off for the boot (kern.sweet_vibrate gave ENODEV).
- Not here: iOS's own haptics and Control Center's flashlight do not reach these sysctls (HakiOS uses userland
  hooks for that).

Checked: syntax-only compile of the changed files against this series' tree (clang, -Wall -Wextra, no warnings);
the driver run in a user-space simulation against a fake PMIC enforcing this patch's whitelist (boot tests,
sysctls, time limit, short circuit, temperature alarm, read-back mismatch, whitelist). On the phone (2026-11-14, local build): without an iOS root (fastboot boot, the boot ends in an initproc panic)
the boot tests lit the flashlight and, with the CFG1 ERM write, buzzed; a panic during the buzz and during the
torch switched each off. With the iOS 18.7.8 root flashed (sweetvib=3 sweettorch=3, over SSH): kern.sweet_torch
500 and 0 light and darken the flashlight, kern.sweet_vibrate 300 and 600 buzz, kern.sweet_vibtorch_state reports
the haptics block as type 0x15 subtype 0x4 and the flash module as type 0x18 subtype 0x4, nothing latched.

